messages:
sarah (support): Hey team - customer (jason) is seeing intermittent 401s only on retried requests from a Lambda. Original request succeeds, retry sometimes ends up unauthenticated. Happening with our TS Node SDK used from a serverless function.

jason (customer): repro steps I gave: Node 18, ts-sdk 1.x, using our token refresh that fetches a new access token when 401, then retries original POST. Sometimes the retried POST goes out with empty Authorization header. Happens ~1 in 10 retries.

vikram (eng-runtime): hmm. first suspicion: AbortSignal reuse or timing with token refresh. Are you reusing an AbortController across attempts?

jason (customer): we do use a shared controller per request lifecycle and call controller.abort() on timeout. retry path reuses the same fetch options (we just swap the header).

maya (devex): repeating: never reuse an AbortController for a fresh attempt. create a new one per fetch. reusing can lead to surprising state if the original was aborted while token refresh was awaited.

sarah (support): can you paste simplified snippet of the retry logic? even pseudo-code is fine.

jason (customer): posted small case: ```ts
async function fetchWithRetry(url, opts) {
  const controller = opts.controller || new AbortController();
  try {
    return await fetch(url, {...opts, signal: controller.signal});
  } catch (e) {
    if (is401(e)) {
      await refreshToken(); // swaps global auth header
      // reuse same opts and controller here 
      return await fetch(url, {...opts, signal: controller.signal});
    }
    throw e;
  }
}
```

vikram (eng-runtime): yep that's the pattern that can leak an aborted signal into the retry. If controller.signal.aborted === true, fetch will short-circuit. But weird you see empty Authorization header intermittently — that suggests timing where headers are mutated before the second fetch but the request that goes out loses header or some agent-level caching is happening.

maya (devex): also ask: which undici/node-fetch are you using under the hood? Node's global fetch vs undici Dispatcher differences matter for signal/agent semantics in serverless.

jason (customer): using node-stdlib fetch (Node 18.17) with undici pinned by ts-sdk. I saw undici@4.x in lockfile.

vikram (eng-runtime): older undici versions had some edge cases with streaming headers and signal propagation when a request was aborted and reused by a pool. Two immediate troubleshooting steps: 1) ensure you create a fresh AbortController per attempt; 2) avoid mutating the same headers object in-place between attempts — clone headers when retrying.

maya (devex): quick patch example: ```ts
async function fetchWithRetry(url, baseOpts) {
  for (let i=0;i<3;i++) {
    const controller = new AbortController();
    const headers = new Headers(baseOpts.headers || {}); // clone
    if (i>0) headers.set('Authorization', await refreshToken());
    try {
      return await fetch(url, {...baseOpts, headers, signal: controller.signal});
    } catch (e) {
      if (shouldRetry(e)) await backoffDelay(i); else throw e;
    }
  }
}
```

jason (customer): okay i'll try cloning headers and new controller. couple Qs: 1) we also saw duplicate POSTs in a different flow — could the retry timing plus signal reuse cause duplicate outbound?

vikram (eng-runtime): yes: if your retry logic starts a second fetch before the first is fully cleaned up (and you mutated shared body/stream) you can end up with duplicate sends. For POSTs with streaming bodies, recommend buffering or respawning a fresh ReadableStream per attempt.

maya (devex): also check for timer drift in Lambda: short-lived functions + event loop freeze can make backoff timers pile up. Use randomized jitter on backoff and cap simultaneous retry attempts.

sarah (support): can you run a small repro we can share? we have a lambda simulator script you can run locally with env to mimic cold start jitter. want to attach?

jason (customer): i'll try tonight and post results. one more: any quick SDK-side mitigations? a config flag?

vikram (eng-runtime): we can add a safeguard in SDK: ensure retries create fresh controllers, deep-clone headers, and if body is a non-repeatable stream, skip auto-retry by default. We don't have a toggle exposed right now but that's a reasonable opt-in. I'll open an issue.

maya (devex): for immediate workaround: upgrade undici if your lockfile allows (newer undici has improved signal handling) and implement the per-attempt controller/headers clone. Also log header values right before fetch to catch mutated/empty token.

jason (customer): thanks — will try clone + new controller + update undici and report back. appreciated! :raised_hands:

vikram (eng-runtime): thanks, filing internal ticket to track SDK changes and a short PR to make retries safer for POSTs. tagging sre-bot for observability if we need to parse logs.

sre-bot: noted. created incident-obs ticket #INC-2026 to add sampling for retry/auth header snapshots in prod.

sarah (support): great, keeping this thread open. please paste logs if you get a repro and we'll escalate to eng if needed. :)
